# Agent guide — {{.Name}}

You are working inside a **browserscale.cloud** automation module written in Go.
It implements the `module.Module` interface from **browserscale-kit** and drives
a cloud browser through **browserscale-go**.

- `flow.go` — thin dispatcher: `doTask(...)` picks which named flow to run.
  Keep it short. Do **not** dump the whole browser script here.
{{- if or (eq .Kind "queue") (eq .Kind "one-shot")}}
- `register.go` — **your main job for now**: one named flow (`register`). Ships
  with a COMPLETE working playground signup — read it first, it's the fastest
  way in. Add further flows as sibling files (`enter.go`, `login.go`, …).
{{- else}}
- `task.go` — **your main job for now**: one named flow (`runTask`). Ships with
  a COMPLETE working playground example — read it first. Rename/split into
  `register.go` / `enter.go` / … as soon as you have more than one flow.
{{- end}}
- `module.go` — identity + the config schema in `Schema()` (edited in a TUI).
- `run.go` — the `{{.Kind}}` lifecycle: `Run(ctx, env)`. Your code — edit freely.
- `rent.go` — proxy loading + rent-with-backoff. Every run is proxied:
  `Run` calls `m.loadProxies(env)` once, then `m.rent(ctx)` draws a random
  proxy from the pool per unit of work. Don't rent without a proxy. Bare
  rent is enough: country/timezone auto-match the proxy exit IP — do **not**
  add `WithCountryCode` / `WithTimezone` unless you mean to override that.
- `main.go` — hands off to `harness.Run` (do not overthink it).
- `data/` — `config.json` (one config), operator input files (`proxies.txt`,
  CSVs, …), and `store.db`. Resolve inputs with
  `filepath.Join(env.FilesDirectory, cfg.SomeFile)`.
- `runs/<name>/` — per-run outputs via `env.Output(...)`.

### Running (and iterating)

```bash
go run .                  # interactive once: edit + save data/config.json
browserscale dev          # preferred while iterating: rebuild → restart → stream
browserscale dev -run dig # same, fixed runs/dig/ folder (append OK)
```

`dev` is always headless (needs `data/config.json`). Without `-run` the harness
creates `runs/<timestamp>/`. Do **not** hand-roll `go build` / Kill /
`Start-Process` loops and do **not** pass `--` / `-yes` to `dev` — it already
starts the module non-interactively.

Stdout/stderr are streamed into the terminal; Cursor/Codex read that live
output. Watch success/retry/inspect/fail lines there (the status line also
updates the terminal title, which agents usually do not see).

### File layout for browser flows

One named flow per file. `doTask` only dispatches:

```go
// flow.go — keep this thin
func (m *Module) doTask(ctx context.Context, browser *browserscale.CloudBrowser{{if eq .Kind "queue"}}, acc account{{end}}) error {
    // pick the flow from config / CSV / flags as the module grows
    return m.register(ctx, browser{{if eq .Kind "queue"}}, acc{{end}})
    // return m.enter(ctx, browser{{if eq .Kind "queue"}}, acc{{end}})
}
```

```text
register.go   →  func (m *Module) register(...)   // signup
enter.go      →  func (m *Module) enter(...)      // raffle / drop entry
login.go      →  func (m *Module) login(...)      // session restore
```

Shared helpers (SMS client, address pool, selectors) get their own files too —
`sms.go`, `address.go` — not stuffed into a flow. A flow file that grows past
~200 lines usually wants another split (phase methods on the same file are
fine; a second *flow* is a second file).

`doTask` / named flows are **pure browser work**: config is on the receiver
(`m.cfg`). The kit services (`env.Logger`, `env.Store`, `env.Output`,
`env.FilesDirectory`) live on the `env` passed to `Run` in `run.go` — do
store/output work there, or thread what you need into the named flow.

This file is the source of truth for both the `browserscale-go` SDK and the
`browserscale-kit` helpers. It is precise — follow the signatures exactly.

**Before writing browser code:** read
{{- if or (eq .Kind "queue") (eq .Kind "one-shot")}} `register.go`
{{- else}} `task.go`
{{- end}}
(a complete working example), then keep new flows in their own files.
**Never invent an SDK method or reach for a Playwright-style helper.** This SDK
is more capable than it looks — most "there's no primitive for this" problems
are already solved (JS-expression locators, multi-condition `Wait`, char-by-char
`Fill`). If a method or signature is not in this file, confirm it exists in the
docs bundled offline under `docs/` **before** using it — open the specific page
or grep the folder:

- `docs/api-reference/go.md` — every Go method: signature, params, throws, examples.
- `docs/guides/*.md` — task guides (locators, waiting, interaction, frames, network, capture, dom-mirror, captchas, …).
- `docs/README.md` — index of every bundled page.

```
grep -rin "getobservation\|selectbytext\|waitforanyresponse" docs/
```

Prefer the bundled docs over guessing; a hallucinated method costs a whole
build/run cycle to discover.

### Optional: probe a live session (browserscale MCP)

This file + `docs/` are the *build-time* reference (how to write the code). If
the **browserscale MCP server** is connected, you also have a *live* one: an
interactive mirror of the SDK for poking a real page and reading back its actual
state. Its tools map 1:1 onto the methods below — `observe`→`GetObservation`,
`evaluate`→`Evaluate`, `wait`→`Wait`, `click`/`fill`→`Click`/`Fill`,
`get_cookies`/… — so anything you confirm live drops **verbatim** into your Go.

There's no fixed sequence — reach for whichever tool answers your current
question. Two common entry points:

- **Explore an unfamiliar page**: `rent` (or reuse a session) → `navigate` →
  `wait` for a landmark → `observe` for the lay of the land. Use `evaluate`
  only to verify a durable css/js target you will reuse, or for probes observe
  cannot answer — not for url/title/text dumps. `screenshot` when you need to
  see it; try `click`/`wait` before writing any of it into a named flow file.
- **Investigate a run that just failed**: don't rent — attach to the broken
  session itself. Feed its `sessionId` + `grpcUrl` (log both from your code) to
  any action tool and inspect the live, half-finished state where it died:
  `screenshot`/`observe` to see where it actually is, `evaluate` to check the
  assumption the code made that the page didn't hold up.

You pass only `sessionId` (+ `grpcUrl`); the API key rides the client's
`Authorization` header, never a tool arg. If no MCP is connected, ignore this
and work from
{{- if or (eq .Kind "queue") (eq .Kind "one-shot")}} `register.go`
{{- else}} `task.go`
{{- end}}
+ `docs/`.

