## Mental model

- One `*browserscale.CloudBrowser` == one rented browser context bound to one
  page. You get it already rented inside `doTask`.
- **Every element on the page — in any frame, same-origin or cross-origin,
  nested any depth — is reached by the same calls.** A frame is just an opaque
  `frameId` string you pass to `.InFrame(...)`. There is no second API for
  iframes.
- The core loop of most flows is **`Wait` → act**: wait for one of several
  conditions, branch on which matched, act, repeat.
- `Navigate` returns at **navigation commit**, not when the page is loaded or
  interactive. Always follow it with `Wait` for a known landmark (CSS) or the
  URL/state you want (JS) before observing or clicking.

## Recipes — complete flows to copy

Full, self-contained shapes. Prefer adapting one of these over assembling
primitives from scratch.
{{- if or (eq .Kind "queue") (eq .Kind "one-shot")}}
`register.go` already contains recipe #1 wired up; `flow.go` just calls it.
{{- else}}
`task.go` already contains recipe #1 wired up; `flow.go` just calls it.
{{- end}}
When you add a second flow, put it in its own file and switch in `doTask`.

**1. Fill a form and branch on the outcome**

```go
// login.go — one named flow per file
func (m *Module) login(ctx context.Context, browser *browserscale.CloudBrowser) error {
    if _, err := browser.Navigate(ctx, "https://target.example/login", 30000); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    // Navigate only waited for commit — wait for a known element before acting.
    if _, err := browser.Wait(ctx, browserscale.CSS("#user"), browserscale.Timeout(15000)); err != nil {
        return err
    }
    browser.FillWith(ctx, browserscale.CSS("#user"), "alice", browserscale.FillOpts{ClearFirst: true})
    browser.Fill(ctx, browserscale.CSS("#pass"), "secret")
    browser.Click(ctx, browserscale.CSS(`button[type="submit"]`))

    wr, err := browser.Wait(ctx,
        browserscale.CSS("#dashboard"), // 0 = success
        browserscale.CSS(".login-error"), // 1 = bad creds
        browserscale.Timeout(20000))
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    if wr.Index == 1 {
        return errors.New("login rejected")
    }
    return nil
}
```

**2. Drain a list across workers with de-dup (queue lifecycle)**

`run.go` already loads the CSV into a `*Queue[account]` and pulls one `acc` per
worker. Make it idempotent — check the store *before* renting a browser so a
re-run skips finished accounts (and saves credits):

```go
// in run.go, before the worker loop:
done := store.NewJSON[bool](env.Store.Namespace(m.Name()))

// inside the loop, after acc, ok := q.Next():
if v, seen, _ := done.Get(acc.email); seen && v {
    continue // already processed in a previous run
}
if err := m.runOnce(ctx, acc); err != nil {
    l.Error(acc.email, err)
    continue
}
_ = done.Put(acc.email, true)
env.Output("results.txt", acc.email+" ok", "append")
```

**3. Confirm an outcome by network, not DOM**

More reliable than watching for a redirect: race the real backend call.

```go
browser.Click(ctx, browserscale.CSS("#buy"))
idx, resp, err := browser.WaitForAnyResponse(ctx, 90000, []browserscale.RequestPattern{
    {URL: "*/api/order*"},   // 0 = the call we want
    {URL: "*/api/error*"},   // 1 = a failure signal
})
if err != nil || idx == 1 {
    return errors.New("order failed")
}
_ = resp.Body
```

**4. Solve a captcha mid-flow**

```go
// After the challenge appears; token + cookies are applied server-side.
if _, err := browser.SolveCaptcha(ctx, 60000, 3); err != nil {
    return err
}
```

## Locators — what to target

Build a target with one of four constructors (never construct the struct
directly):

```go
browserscale.CSS("button.submit")   // CSS selector (light DOM only — does NOT pierce shadow roots)
browserscale.JS("<expr returning a DOM element>")  // arbitrary JS; can pierce shadow roots via __wrc.shadow
browserscale.Node(backendNodeId)     // an id from a previous WaitResult/EvaluateResult (actions only)
browserscale.At(x, y)                // viewport coordinates (Click & MoveTo only)
```

Chainable modifiers on a locator:

```go
browserscale.CSS("#banner").Visible(false)   // don't require visibility (wait only; default true)
browserscale.CSS("#banner").Steady(0)         // disable the 500ms steady-position check (wait only)
browserscale.CSS("button").InFrame(frameId)   // scope to one frame
browserscale.CSS("button").InAllFrames()      // search every frame (== .InFrame(browserscale.AllFrames))
```

Rules:
- `Node` and `At` are **not** valid `Wait` conditions (they error). They are
  action targets only.
- `At(x, y)` is accepted **only** by `Click` and `MoveTo`. `Fill`, `ScrollTo`,
  `Drag*`, `SelectOption`, `ReadCanvas` require a real element.

### JS-locator idioms — replace hand-rolled `Evaluate` helpers

The `JS(...)` locator returns *any* element from an arbitrary expression, so the
"there's no CSS for this" helpers you'd write in Playwright are one-liners here.
Target with `JS(...)` and let the SDK do the real click/fill — never
`el.click()` inside `Evaluate` (synthetic events are anti-bot-flagged; see Don't).

```go
// Click by visible text / role (this is your "clickByText" — don't build one):
browser.Click(ctx, browserscale.JS(
    `[...document.querySelectorAll('button,[role=button],a,input[type=submit]')]` +
        `.find(e => /次へ|Next/.test(e.innerText || e.value))`))

// Nth of a duplicated id (CSS "#dup" only ever matches the first one):
browser.Fill(ctx, browserscale.JS(`document.querySelectorAll('input#dup')[3]`), "value")

// Real checkbox hidden behind a styled label — click the label, then verify:
browser.Click(ctx, browserscale.CSS("label[for='agree']"))
if r, _ := browser.Evaluate(ctx, `document.getElementById('agree').checked`); r.Value != true {
    // not ticked — fall back to the wrapping <label> or the input itself
}
```

## Waiting — the primary primitive

```go
wr, err := browser.Wait(ctx,
    browserscale.CSS("#success"),                  // condition index 0
    browserscale.CSS("#error"),                    // condition index 1
    browserscale.JS("!!window.__ready"),           // condition index 2 (JS predicate)
    browserscale.Timeout(20000),                   // optional; default 30000ms
)
// wr.Index      → which condition matched first (argument order)
// wr.FrameId    → the frame it matched in
// wr.BackendNodeId → the matched node (valid only within wr.FrameId)
switch wr.Index {
case 1:
    return errors.New("site showed an error")
}
```

- Multiple conditions race; the **first to match wins** and its index is
  returned. This is *the* way to branch on A/B widgets, error-vs-success pages,
  captcha-vs-no-captcha, etc.
- For a CSS/JS condition the SDK requires the element to be **visible** and
  **position-steady for 500ms** by default. Opt out per condition with
  `.Visible(false)` / `.Steady(0)`.
- A JS condition matches as soon as the expression is **truthy** (visible/steady
  are no-ops unless it returns an Element).
- Don't build wait loops out of `Evaluate` + `time.Sleep`. `Wait` is the tool.
- After every `Navigate`, `Wait` for readiness — commit ≠ loaded. Same for
  clicks that trigger a client-side route change.

Wait for a client-side navigation / redirect the same way — a JS predicate, not
a poll loop. Prefer asserting the **destination** over "the url changed": it also
survives multi-hop redirect chains (OAuth, interstitials) that end where you want.

```go
// Landed on the member page? (works across a login → OAuth → redirect chain)
browser.Wait(ctx, browserscale.JS(`location.pathname.startsWith("/mypage")`))
```

## Actions

All actions return their resolved `FrameId`/`BackendNodeId`, so ids flow into
your script as a side effect.

```go
browser.Click(ctx, target)                                   // human-like move + full mouseDown/Up
browser.ClickWith(ctx, target, browserscale.ClickOpts{Button: "right", ClickCount: 2})
browser.Fill(ctx, target, "text")                            // clicks, focuses, types char-by-char (appends)
browser.FillWith(ctx, target, "text", browserscale.FillOpts{ClearFirst: true})  // wipe field first
browser.SelectByValue(ctx, target, "DE")                     // also SelectByIndex / SelectByText (+ *With + SelectOpts{NoEvents})
browser.ScrollTo(ctx, target)                                // bring element into view (walks nested/OOPIF scrollers)
browser.MoveTo(ctx, target)                                  // hover (At allowed)
browser.DragBy(ctx, target, dx, dy)                          // press, drag along human path, release
browser.DragTo(ctx, target, absX, absY)                      // drag to absolute root-viewport coords
```

Keyboard (targets whatever is focused):

```go
browser.PressKey(ctx, "Enter", "Enter", 0, 0)   // key, code, modifiers(Alt=1,Ctrl=2,Meta=4,Shift=8), location
browser.ReleaseKey(ctx, "Enter", "Enter", 0, 0)
browser.InsertText(ctx, "pasted text")           // IME-style commit at caret
```

**Typing that must enable a `disabled` button:** use `Fill`. It types
character-by-character and fires real key events, so an SPA's client-side
validators run and flip a gated "next"/"submit" button to enabled. **Don't**
reach for `InsertText` here — it's a bulk IME commit with **no per-key events**,
so a button gated on real input stays disabled and you'll waste time re-typing.

`*Opts.InFrame` (Click/Fill/Select*) overrides the locator's frame — handy to
reuse one base locator across frames. `MoveTo`/`ScrollTo`/`Drag*` take the frame
from the locator itself.

## Evaluate — arbitrary JS

```go
r, err := browser.Evaluate(ctx, "document.title")        // main frame
r, err := browser.EvaluateInFrame(ctx, frameId, "location.href")  // a specific frame
```

Two result shapes on the single `EvaluateResult`:
- **Value path** — a JSON value → `r.Value` (`string`/`float64`/`bool`/`[]any`/`map[string]any`/`nil`). Type-assert it: `n, _ := r.Value.(float64)`.
- **Element path** — the expression returned a DOM Element → `r.Value` is `nil`
  but `r.BackendNodeId` is non-zero. Act on it with `browserscale.Node(r.BackendNodeId)`.

Rules:
- **Single expression only.** Multi-statement bodies need an IIFE:
  `(() => { ...; return x; })()`.
- A thrown JS error surfaces as a Go error on the call.
- Don't use `Evaluate` to click (`el.click()`) — it fires synthetic events with
  no mouse path and anti-bot stacks flag it. Use `Click`.

## Scripts — move a chatty loop into the browser

`RunScript` sends JavaScript to run *in the browser process*, with a `browser`
object exposing the same operations as this SDK. Each call inside the script is a
local function call, not a gRPC round trip, so a step costs microseconds instead
of tens of milliseconds.

```go
result, err := browser.RunScript(ctx, `
    const out = [];
    for (let page = 1; page <= 20; page++) {
        await browser.click({ selector: ".next" });
        await browser.wait({ selector: ".row" });
        for (const el of await browser.getDOM().querySelectorAll(".row")) {
            out.push(el.textContent);
        }
    }
    return out;
`)
if err != nil { return err }          // the script could not be delivered
if !result.Success {                  // the script threw; the message is in Result
    return fmt.Errorf("scrape failed: %s", result.Result)
}
```

`result.Result` is the return value as JSON; `result.Log` is everything the script
printed. Note the two failure modes: a Go error means the call did not happen, a
`Success == false` means it ran and threw.

**When to reach for it.** Only when the round trips are what costs you: a poll
loop, a walk over many elements, pagination. A flow of a dozen clicks is clearer
as Go, and Go is where you have types, the kit, and a debugger. Moving a whole
flow into a string is a step backwards.

**Long-running work** uses `StartScript`, which returns a run id at once and
streams output to a handler, with `run.Wait(ctx)` for the outcome and
`run.Stop(ctx)` to cancel. `RunScript` cannot be cancelled — its run id only
arrives with the reply — so use `StartScript` for anything that might not finish.

## Frames & iframes

`frameId` is an opaque string; everything takes it uniformly.

```go
// Don't know which frame? Fan out, then act in the frame it matched.
r, _ := browser.Wait(ctx, browserscale.CSS("button.accept-all").InAllFrames())
browser.Click(ctx, browserscale.Node(r.BackendNodeId).InFrame(r.FrameId))

// Explicit tree (rarely needed in the hot path):
pages, _ := browser.GetPages(ctx)   // []*PageInfo, each with a recursive FrameTree (FrameInfo)
```

`FrameInfo` fields you'll use: `FrameId`, `Url`, `IsOOPIF`, `HasJSContext`
(false → `EvaluateInFrame` will fail), `IsVisible`, `AbsoluteRect`
(root-viewport coords, even for deep OOPIFs — use for `Click(At(x,y))`).

Gotchas:
- `browserscale.AllFrames` is the sentinel `"ALL_FRAMES"`, not a real id.
  `EvaluateInFrame` does **not** accept it — discover the frame with
  `Wait(...).InAllFrames()` first, then evaluate in `r.FrameId`.
- A navigation mints a **new** `frameId`; a `backendNodeId` is valid only
  paired with the `FrameId` from the same result. Don't carry either across
  navigations.

## Reading the page

```go
obs, _ := browser.GetObservation(ctx)          // FIRST look at any page, and the cheapest re-read of its state
rev, _ := browser.GetDomRevision(ctx, "")      // O(1) mutation counter - poll this to ask "did the document move?"
dom, _ := browser.GetDOM(ctx, "", -1)          // full CDP DOM.Node JSON, one snapshot (depth -1 = whole tree)
hash, _ := browser.GetDOMHash(ctx, "")         // sha256[:8] - only to compare *content*, not to detect movement
shot, _ := browser.Screenshot(ctx, "png", 0)   // shot.DataBase64 / Width / Height

// Follow the DOM instead of re-reading it: ONE live tree for the whole page,
// reporting changes only inside what you expanded, so a subtree nobody opened
// costs a child count instead of a re-serialized document. An <iframe> is an
// ordinary element whose one child is the document it hosts. Replaces a
// GetDOMHash + GetDOM polling loop. See docs/guides/dom-mirror.md.
mirror, _ := browser.MirrorDom(ctx, browserscale.DomMirrorOptions{}, func(m *browserscale.DomMirror) {
    render(m.Root())                           // "something moved, re-read the root"
}, nil)
defer mirror.Stop(ctx)

// Narrow it when a page is huge, ask for JSON, or re-read one subtree:
obs, _ = browser.GetObservationWith(ctx, browserscale.ObservationOpts{ViewportOnly: true})
obs, _ = browser.GetObservationWith(ctx, browserscale.ObservationOpts{Format: "json"})
obs, _ = browser.GetObservationWith(ctx, browserscale.ObservationOpts{Selector: "form#register"})
```

`GetObservation` returns text meant to be read as-is. Per frame it emits header
lines, then one line per visible element:

```
# frame 8F03BF2EEB5ADC9CE47B15775289BBD3 https://example.com/register
# title "Account registration"
# scroll y=1200/8400
input#email[47] type="email" name="loginId" value="a@b.com" required click "E-Mail"
input#agree[51] type="checkbox" checked="false" required click "Accept terms"
button[70] type="submit" click "Continue"
```

- **Don't re-derive state with `Evaluate`.** URL, title, scroll offset, live
  input values, checkbox state and duplicate-id flags are all in there already.
  `value=` is the live property (so it proves a `Fill` landed; passwords show as
  a length), and the trailing quoted string is always the label — never the
  value — so an empty and a prefilled field stay distinguishable.
- **Flags:** `click` = interactive, `offscreen` = `ScrollTo` it first,
  `disabled`/`required`/`readonly`/`selected`, `hidden="reason"` = interactive
  but not seeable, `id-not-unique` / `id-not-selectable` = the `#id` selector is
  ambiguous or points elsewhere.
- **Picking a target:** `backendNodeId` (the `47`) works via `Node(47)` but dies
  with the document — useless in the code you're writing. Prefer `name`, then an
  unflagged id, then a text/label relation via `JS(...)`, never a generated class
  name. Act through that durable target during exploration and every call that
  worked is already a line of your script; actions return the `backendNodeId`
  they hit, so compare it against the observation to prove the anchor is right.
- **`# budget spent`** means the rest of that frame degraded to interactive
  elements only — scroll and observe again, or use `ViewportOnly`.
- **Subtree scope:** after the first full look, pass `Selector` /
  `JSExpression` / `BackendNodeId` (+ `InFrame` when needed) so follow-up
  looks cost the form, not the ads around it. Child iframes inside the
  scope are still visited.

## Captcha & tainted canvas

```go
// Detects & solves the first supported challenge; token/cookies applied server-side.
_, err := browser.SolveCaptcha(ctx, 60000, 3)   // timeoutMs (0=60s default), retryAmount

// Read a <canvas> even if cross-origin/tainted (toDataURL would throw). Bypasses page JS.
res, _ := browser.ReadCanvas(ctx, browserscale.CSS("#chart canvas"))         // res.DataBase64, Width, Height, OriginClean
res, _ := browser.ReadCanvasWith(ctx, browserscale.CSS("canvas"),
    browserscale.ReadCanvasOpts{Format: "jpeg", Quality: 80, SW: 150, SH: 300}) // Format: png|jpeg|webp|rgba; SX/SY/SW/SH in canvas pixels
```

## Shadow DOM — `__wrc.shadow`

CSS selectors don't cross shadow boundaries. Inside any `Evaluate`/`Wait`/JS
locator, the native helper `__wrc.shadow(host)` returns a host's shadow root
(open **or closed**). It's a function-scoped local, never a `window` global.

```go
// Wait for something behind a closed shadow root:
browser.Wait(ctx, browserscale.JS(`!!__wrc.shadow(document.querySelector("my-widget"))?.querySelector(".ready")`))

// Act on an element inside it — JS-expression targeting resolves & clicks it for real:
browser.Click(ctx, browserscale.JS(`__wrc.shadow(document.querySelector("my-widget")).querySelector("button.accept")`))
```

Works for `Click`, `Fill`, `ScrollTo`, `SelectOption`, `Drag*`, `ReadCanvas`.
The manual `Click(At(x,y))` detour is only for targets inside a **cross-origin
iframe** (see `FrameInfo.AbsoluteRect`).

## Network

```go
browser.SetBlockList(ctx, []string{"*.doubleclick.net/*", "*googletagmanager.com*"})  // drop matching requests
browser.SetStaticPaths(ctx, "snap-id", []string{"*.example.com/*"})                   // serve cached assets (saves bandwidth/credits)

// Race a real backend request against another action — more reliable than a DOM redirect.
idx, req, err := browser.WaitForAnyRequest(ctx, 90000, []browserscale.RequestPattern{
    {URL: "*/api/success*"},
    {URL: "*/error*", Abort: true},   // Abort drops it with an empty 200
})
idx, resp, err := browser.WaitForAnyResponse(ctx, 90000, patterns)  // resp.StatusCode / Body / Headers
req, err := browser.ModifyRequest(ctx, "*/api/me", "", 5000, []browserscale.HeaderModification{
    {Action: browserscale.HeaderModificationAdd, Name: "X-Trace", Value: "abc"},
})

// Log EVERY request the session completes, instead of trapping one. Runs in the
// browser process, so cross-process iframes, workers and each hop of a redirect
// chain are included; nothing is ever paused. See docs/guides/capture.md.
capture, _ := browser.CaptureNetwork(ctx, browserscale.NetworkCaptureOptions{
    Patterns: []string{"*/api/*"},              // prefix a pattern with "!" to exclude it
    Bodies:   browserscale.NetworkBodiesText,   // "none" (default) / text / all
}, func(ex browserscale.NetworkExchange) {
    log.Println(ex.StatusCode, ex.Method, ex.Url) // NEVER block in here; hand slow work to a channel
})
defer capture.Stop(ctx)                          // a cancelled ctx alone leaves it armed
```

## Cookies & storage

```go
cookies, _ := browser.GetCookies(ctx)                 // []CookieParam
browser.SetCookies(ctx, []browserscale.CookieParam{
    {Name: "a", Value: "b", Domain: "example.com", Path: "/"},
})
browser.ClearCookies(ctx)
storage, _ := browser.GetStorage(ctx, "")             // localStorage grouped by origin; feed back verbatim to SetStorage
browser.SetStorage(ctx, storage)
```

Session metadata getters: `browser.CountryCode()`, `browser.Timezone()`,
`browser.AcceptLanguage()`, `browser.Fingerprint()`, `browser.SessionId()`.

## Proven patterns

- **Branch on A/B / success-vs-fail** with a multi-condition `Wait` and
  `switch wr.Index` — never guess which variant rendered. After a click that
  should leave the page, include a "navigated away" condition (e.g.
  `JS(\`location.pathname !== ${strconv.Quote(from)}\`)`) in the race so the
  *old* page can't match again immediately.
- **Unknown page while mapping**: `GetObservation(ctx)`, log it, return
  `ErrInspect` (defined in `run.go` for queue modules) so the status line
  shows Inspect and you can attach to the live session — don't invent the
  next click.
- **Confirm outcome by network**, not DOM: after a submit/captcha, race
  `WaitForAnyRequest` on the real backend endpoints against your other steps.
- **Locate-then-act**: one `Evaluate`/`Wait` returns a `BackendNodeId`; act with
  `browserscale.Node(id)` — no second lookup, survives tricky selectors.
- **On a new/unknown page, observe first**: `GetObservation(ctx)` is your cheap
  first look and your cheap re-read after every step; also log it when a
  selector breaks. On click/fill failure, observe before writing more JS.
- **One file per named flow**: `register.go` / `enter.go` / `login.go`. Keep
  `doTask` as the dispatcher — never grow a second flow inside the same file.
- **Bare rent + proxy**: leave country/timezone unset so they match the proxy
  exit IP (same as MCP `rent`). Overrides are for intentional mismatch only.
- **Save credits**: `SetBlockList` heavy trackers / `SetStaticPaths` frozen
  assets on flows that reload a lot.
- **Retry / Inspect outcomes**: return `ErrRetry` or `ErrInspect` from the
  flow when those sentinels exist in `run.go` — the queue status line counts
  them separately from plain failures. To retry a specific step, loop inside
  the flow; to retry a whole item, re-enqueue it.

## Don't

- Don't dump the whole automation into `flow.go` / `doTask` — dispatch to named
  flow files instead.
- Don't `time.Sleep` where a `Wait(...)` condition works.
- Don't drive clicks/typing through `Evaluate` (`el.click()`, `el.value = "..."`)
  — use `Click` / `Fill` / `InsertText`.
- Don't `evaluate` DOM surgery (`.remove()` on headers/overlays) to "help"
  clicks — `Click` already evades sticky/fixed occluders; if still blocked,
  `observe`, then dismiss via a real close control or scroll, and retry.
- Don't re-derive page state with `Evaluate` (`document.title`, `location.href`,
  `body.innerText`, hand-rolled input maps) — `GetObservation` already has it.
- Don't add `WithCountryCode` / `WithTimezone` on every rent "to be safe".
- Don't carry a `backendNodeId` or `frameId` across a navigation.
